s and heritage records for an estimated 900 individuals, which is personally significant but limited in nationwide economic scale.","evidence":"An estimated 900 American-Jewish servicemembers of the Armed Forces, killed in World War I and World War II and buried overseas in United States military cemeteries were, for various reasons, mistakenly buried under Latin Crosses.","evidence_verified":true,"key":"magnitude","score":40},{"assessment":"Authorizes a specific contract amount of $500,000 annually for the nonprofit organization carrying out the program.","evidence":"Each contract under this subsection shall be for one year and in the amount of $500,000 to the nonprofit organization.","evidence_verified":true,"key":"fiscal","score":20},{"assessment":"Directs a single commission to establish a temporary restoration program and makes a minor technical extension to pension payment limits.","evidence":"The American Battle Monuments Commission shall establish a program to identify covered members and to contact survivors and descendants","evidence_verified":true,"key":"scope","score":30},{"assessment":"The program is carried out during the first five fiscal years following enactment.","evidence":"The Commission shall carry out the Fallen Servicemembers Religious Heritage Restoration Program during the first five fiscal years that begin after the date of the enactment of this Act.","evidence_verified":true,"key":"duration","score":30}],"missing_information":["Specific administrative costs absorbed directly by the American Battle Monuments Commission outside of the contractor grants."],"rationale":"The bill creates a specific temporary program through the American Battle Monuments Commission and extends a pension limitation date by two months, representing limited overall scope and fiscal impact.","score":30},"importance_meta":{"generated_at":"2026-10-25T19:30:20+00:00","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","source_basis":"text+summary","source_urls":["https://www.congress.gov/